<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Spanish Oran</h1><p class="st-meta">1509 CE – 1792 CE · capital Oran</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spain's fortress city on the Algerian coast, held for nearly three centuries as a frontier presidio facing the Ottoman Regency of Algiers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mers-el-kebir">Mers el-Kébir</a> in 1505, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> in 1509 in an expedition financed and led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-jimenez-de-cisneros">Cardinal Cisneros</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-navarro">Pedro Navarro</a> commanding the troops. The conquest continued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and aimed to stop raids on the Spanish coast and control the trade of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zayyanid-kingdom-of-tlemcen">Zayyanid Kingdom of Tlemcen</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> was a garrison town ruled by captains-general, ringed with forts and supplied from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Its governors allied with or raided neighbouring tribes and played politics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>. The Count of Alcaudete, governor from 1534, died in a failed attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mostaganem">Mostaganem</a> in 1558. Oran's Jewish community was expelled in 1669, and the town also served as a penal settlement.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Bey of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beylik-of-the-west">Beylik of the West</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> in 1708, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> recaptured it in 1732. After a devastating earthquake in 1790, Spain negotiated its handover to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers</a>, completed in 1792.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/spanish-oran.jpg" alt="Spanish Oran" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ningyou. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cross_of_Burgundy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1509 CE – 1792 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Oran</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Count of Alcaudete</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oran and Mers el-Kébir</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1650&amp;focus=spanish-oran" aria-label="Spanish Oran on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4664 888 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Spanish Oran lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4984 1144L4885 950M4984 1144L4910 989M4984 1144L5077 1108"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4885 950h0M4910 989h0M5077 1108h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4984 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4984 1144h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Oran. <a href="/atlas?year=1650&amp;focus=spanish-oran">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

